WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
RYAN MATSUMOTO: From health
care to finance to social media,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.440
data is helping businesses
innovate to a degree that

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.240
was once deemed unimaginable.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.875
But at the same time, data
also presents new challenges

00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:12.000
to the developers who are
trying to make it useful.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.192
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.302 --> 00:00:21.010
There are many different
factors to think

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.680
about when choosing a database,
especially when you're dealing

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.750
with large amounts of data.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.460
Maybe you're building
a music application

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.340
that aims to recommend songs to
its users based on other music

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.870
that they like.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:34.450
Initially, your app may
simply recommend the top songs

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.903
to users based on the genre
they listen to the most.

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:39.820
But what if you want to
take it a step further

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.490
and provide more personalized
song recommendations using

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:43.880
machine learning?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.030
What if your app went viral?

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.600
These situations present new
challenges to your developers.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.420
The scale of your
existing database

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.920
limits what you can do because
of the massive amount of data

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.920
required to power your music
personalization algorithm.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.860
Where can you find a database
that scales easily, allows

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.510
for large numbers
of reads and writes

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.730
per second at low
latency, and comes

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.190
in a fully-managed service?

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.130
This is where Cloud Bigtable
table comes into play.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.190
Bigtable is Google Cloud's
fully-managed, horizontally

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.770
scalable, NoSQL database
that is designed

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.460
to support large analytical
and operational workloads.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.050
It's optimized for
applications that

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.060
require large numbers
of reads and writes

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.600
per second, low latency,
and performance at scale.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.400
Let's take a look at several of
the key advantages of Bigtable.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.490
First, Bigtable is
fast and performant.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.290
It supports large
numbers of reads

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.600
and writes, with the
ability to support millions

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.550
of requests per second.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.710
And it's able to do
so with low latency,

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.770
on the order of
single-digit milliseconds.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.810
One potential
application for Bigtable

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.940
could be collecting
global air quality data

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.920
from thousands of sensors.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.590
Bigtable can handle the massive
write throughput generated

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.940
and also be able to read that
data quickly and reliably.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.060
Bigtable is also a
highly scalable database.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.890
Throughput can be
adjusted dynamically

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.530
by adding or removing
Bigtable nodes.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.870
A node is just a set
of compute resources

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.780
that provides up to 10,000
additional queries per second

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.150
and is attached to
additional storage capacity.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.640
There's no downtime when you
change the number of nodes,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.370
so you can seamlessly
scale up to meet

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.380
the demands of a
large batch workload

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.680
and scale back down
once it's complete.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.720
Thanks to the
scalability, Bigtable

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.280
allows you to store up
to petabytes of data.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.940
This can be helpful if your
application is growing rapidly

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.590
and you need to scale quickly to
meet the demands of new users.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:39.350
Bigtable is also a fully-managed
and reliable database.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.950
This allows you to concentrate
on building your application,

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.730
rather than spending a lot of
time fine-tuning the database.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.430
Data replication across
multiple Google data centers

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.400
ensures high availability
and low latency for users

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.270
all around the world.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.110
In fact, Bigtable recently
joined Cloud Firestore

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.840
and Cloud Spanner with up
to 99.999% availability SLA.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.750
You can replicate your data
by creating new clusters.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.620
A cluster is a Bigtable
service in a specific location.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.490
Bigtable is
eventually consistent.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.412
So when you write a
change to one cluster,

00:03:12.412 --> 00:03:13.870
you will eventually
be able to read

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.660
that change from other clusters,
but only after the change

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.580
is replicated.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.220
Bigtable also handles
backups, so you can easily

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.100
recover from application-level
data corruption, as well

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.150
as human errors.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.180
Bigtable integrates easily
with popular big data tools,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.070
making it easy for development
teams to get started.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.090
It has support for the
open-source HBase API standard,

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.860
making it easy to integrate
with the Apache ecosystem,

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:40.720
including HBase, Beam,
Hadoop, and Spark.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.000
Bigtable also integrates
well with other Google Cloud

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.700
big data products, like
BigQuery, Dataflow,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.040
and Dataproc.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.745
While Cloud Bigtable makes
sense for many use cases,

00:03:50.745 --> 00:03:52.870
there are situations where
a different Google Cloud

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.120
database may make more sense.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.670
Spanner is preferable if
you need strong consistency

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.520
globally, which makes it
better for transactions.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.770
It's also a SQL
database, which is better

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.990
for more structured data.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.450
BigQuery is an
enterprise data warehouse

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.910
optimized for gaining
organizational insights

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.910
from large amounts of
relational structured data.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.650
Bigtable, on the
other hand, is better

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.839
for applications that
need high numbers of reads

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.940
and writes for second.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.970
Cloud Firestore is Google
Cloud's serverless, NoSQL,

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.980
document-based
database that makes

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.260
it particularly easy to
integrate with mobile and web

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.160
apps.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.290
It's a good choice if
you need a flexible data

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.410
model with a guarantee
of strong consistency.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.960
Let's learn more about
Bigtable's data model.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.660
Bigtable stores data in
massively scalable tables

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.290
that can scale up to billions of
rows and thousands of columns.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.860
As a NoSQL database, Bigtable
stores data in key-value pairs

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.000
stored in rows and columns.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.060
Each row represents
a single entity,

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.067
while each column contains
values for each row.

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.650
Columns that are
related to each other

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:55.480
can be grouped into
column families

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.180
for organizational purposes.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.880
At the intersection of each
row and column combination,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.820
you can have multiple cells,
with each cell representing

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.850
a different version of the
data at a given timestamp.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.870
This makes Bigtable great
for time series analysis.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.850
Bigtable tables
are sparse, meaning

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.300
that if a column is not
filled in for a given row,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.160
it doesn't take up any space.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.130
If you're looking for a highly
scalable, reliable, and fast

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.380
database to handle
big data workloads,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.570
Bigtable may be just
what your business needs.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.560
Try it out for yourself by going
to cloud.google.com/bigtable,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.760
and stay tuned to hear
more about Bigtable.

